== 1. Raft, one failure at a time

#meta[ · about 0:30 min]

#opening[Hi. We are presenting the Raft paper by Ongaro and Ousterhout. Plan: why the paper exists, then we rebuild Raft live, one failure at a time, then how the authors tested it, their results, and what we think.]

#background[Plan: why the paper exists, then we rebuild Raft live one failure at a time, then how the authors evaluated it, results, and our critique.]

== 2. Consensus, and the algorithm everyone used

#meta[Introduction · Introduction · about 1:00 min]

#opening[Start with the problem the paper attacks.]

#click(1)[Consensus: a group of machines acts as one reliable machine and keeps working when some of them fail.][Consensus: a group of machines that acts as one reliable machine and keeps working when some of them fail. Every replicated database and config store needs this.]
#click(2)[For a decade the answer was Paxos: what courses taught, and the starting point of most implementations.][The standard answer was Paxos. Lamport wrote it around 1989, and for the ten years before this paper it was, in the authors' words, almost synonymous with consensus: what courses taught, and what most implementations started from.]
#click(3)[With good reason: proven safe, available while a majority is up, efficient in the normal case.][And for good reasons: proven safe, keeps working as long as a majority is up, so five servers survive two failures, and efficient in the normal case.]
#click(4)[The catch: its core agrees on one value. A log needs many instances glued together: multi-Paxos.][The catch is what Paxos is at its core. Single-decree Paxos agrees on one value. For a log you run one instance per entry and glue them together: multi-Paxos. The gluing is where the trouble starts.]

#watch[The tiles: 1 value vs multi-Paxos.]

#background[Section 1 and the first paragraph of Section 3. The dates are from the paper's references \[15\] and \[16\]; VR and Zab are from Section 10.]

== 3. What's wrong with Paxos?

#meta[Introduction · Introduction · about 1:30 min]

#opening[The authors see two drawbacks, and the paper is a reaction to both.]

#click(1)[Exceptionally hard to understand. The original is opaque; the simplified explanations cover only single-decree Paxos, and are still hard.][First, exceptionally hard to understand. Lamport's original is famously opaque. The simplified explanations only cover the single-decree part and are still hard. An informal survey at NSDI 2012 found few people comfortable with it, even experienced researchers. The authors themselves needed almost a year, and only got there by designing their own alternative.]
#click(2)[Diagnosis: the single-decree foundation. Two stages with no intuitive explanation that can't be understood separately, then composition rules on top.][Their diagnosis is the foundation: two stages with no intuitive explanation that can't be understood independently, and then multi-Paxos adds composition rules with more subtlety on top.]
#click(3)[A poor foundation for real systems. There is no agreed-upon multi-Paxos: Lamport sketched it and many details are missing.][Second, a poor foundation for real systems. There is no agreed-upon multi-Paxos: Lamport mostly described single-decree and sketched the rest, so details are missing. And the architecture works against you: picking entries independently and merging them adds complexity, and symmetric peers make sense for one decision, but for a series it is simpler and faster to elect a leader first.]
#click(4)[So every implementation starts with Paxos, hits the gaps, and ends up with a different, unproven architecture.][So every implementation starts with Paxos, hits the gaps, and ends up with a significantly different architecture. Slow, error-prone, and the proofs no longer apply to what you built. Google's Chubby team said exactly this. Point at the quote.]

#watch[The Chubby quote.]

#background[Section 3. The architecture points: choosing entries independently then merging adds complexity; symmetric peers make sense for one decision, but for a series it is simpler to elect a leader first.]

== 4. Understandability as the primary goal

#meta[Introduction · Introduction · about 1:00 min]

#opening[That is the motivation. The research question follows directly from it.]

#click(1)[Research question: can we define a consensus algorithm for practical systems and describe it so that it is significantly easier to learn than Paxos?][Can we define a consensus algorithm for practical systems and describe it so that it is significantly easier to learn than Paxos?]
#click(2)[Not just an algorithm that works: it should be obvious why it works.][Not just an algorithm that works. In their words, it had to be obvious why it works, because system builders need those intuitions. Point at the quote.]
#click(3)[Every design decision was judged by how hard it is to explain. The authors admit this is subjective, so they measure it later.][Understandability became the test for every design decision: how hard is it to explain, how complex is its state space, does it have subtle implications? They admit this is subjective, which is why they measure it with a user study later.]
#click(4)[Key results: 33 of 43 students did better on Raft; Raft is formally specified and proven safe; it is as efficient as Paxos.][Key results up front: 43 students learned both, 33 did better on Raft. Raft is formally specified and proven safe. And it is as efficient as Paxos. We come back to all three.]

#background[Sections 1 and 4. Key results up front (the tips ask for this); the details come in Results.]

== 5. How that shaped Raft

#meta[Methods · design · Methods · about 1:00 min]

#opening[Two techniques turned that goal into a design.]

#click(1)[Decomposition: Paxos's stages can't be understood separately, so Raft splits consensus into pieces that can.][Decomposition. Paxos's two stages can't be understood separately, so Raft splits consensus into pieces that can: leader election, log replication, safety, and membership changes. Point at the three pillars. You can learn each one on its own, and that is how we present it today.]
#click(2)[Shrink the state space: fewer ways for servers to disagree, fewer cases to reason about. Three answers to Paxos:][Reducing the state space: fewer ways for servers to disagree, so fewer cases to think about. Three examples, each a direct answer to Paxos. Point at the table.]
#click(3)[A strong leader: log entries only flow from the leader to the followers.][A strong leader: entries only flow from the leader to the followers. Paxos has symmetric peers and only suggests a leader as an optimization.]
#click(4)[Logs without holes, appended in order, with only a few allowed ways for two logs to differ.][Logs without holes, appended in order, and only a few allowed ways for two logs to differ. Paxos picks each slot independently and merges afterwards.]
#click(5)[Randomness where it simplifies: random election timeouts, chosen over ranking the candidates.][Randomness where it simplifies: election timeouts are random, choose any, it doesn't matter. They considered ranking candidates instead and dropped it for subtle availability problems.]
#click(6)[Next we rebuild Raft from nothing, one failure at a time. Every rule we add is from the paper.][That is my part. From here on the simulator does the talking: we rebuild Raft from nothing, one failure at a time. Every rule we add is from the paper, and the sidebar on the right collects them.]

#watch[Pillars, then the Paxos \/ Raft table row by row.]

#background[Section 4, plus Section 1's list of novel features and Section 5.2 for the rejected ranking scheme (it had subtle availability issues). The live demo is our way of presenting Sections 5–8.]

== 6. A replicated, deterministic log

#meta[Methods · the artifact, rebuilt live · The goal · about 1:00 min]

#opening[Five servers, each with a log and a tiny key-value store.]

#click(1)[Five servers run the same deterministic state machine: a tiny key–value store.][Deterministic means: same input, same output. No randomness, no clocks inside the state machine.]
#click(2)[Every command goes into a log; each server applies its log in order.][Watch the client send three writes. Each server appends them and applies them in order.]
#click(3)[Same commands in the same order ⇒ same state on every server.][So the whole problem becomes: agree on the log.]
#click(4)[Goal: keep working while any minority of servers is down or unreachable.][And we want to survive failures. With five servers, any two can be down.]

#watch[The logs table and the state machines filling up identically.]

#background[Deterministic is the key word: if the input sequence is identical, the output is identical. So the whole problem reduces to agreeing on the log.]

== 7. Everyone accepts writes

#meta[Methods · the artifact, rebuilt live · Problem · about 1:00 min]

#opening[First idea: let every server accept writes.]

#click(1)[Two clients write the same key at the same moment.][Client A on the left writes x\=1, client B on the right writes x\=2, at the same time.]
#click(2)[Messages travel different distances, so servers see them in different orders…][Messages travel different distances, so the left servers get A first and the right servers get B first.]
#click(3)[…and end up with different values for x. The replicas have diverged.][Point at the red values: half the servers think x\=1, the others x\=2. Nobody did anything wrong.]
#click(4)[Fix: let one server, the leader, decide the order of everything.][So someone has to decide the order. That's the leader.]

#watch[Left vs right servers; red cells in the state machines.]

#background[Point at the left servers vs the right servers: left got A first, right got B first. Nobody did anything wrong. We need someone to decide the order.]

== 8. Why a majority? #opt

#meta[Methods · the artifact, rebuilt live · Background · about 1:00 min]

#opening[Optional slide. Skip it if short on time.]

#click(1)[Decisions need a majority: 3 of 5 servers.][Every decision in Raft needs three of five.]
#click(2)[Any two majorities overlap in at least one server…][Blue is one majority, orange is another. They always share at least one server, here S3.]
#click(3)[…and that server remembers the first decision, so a conflicting second one can't also win.][That shared server is why two conflicting decisions can't both win. Every safety rule later uses this.]
#click(4)[5 servers survive 2 failures: 3 left is still a majority.][Two servers crash. Three are left, still a majority, so the cluster keeps working.]

#background[Blue majority S1–S3, orange majority S3–S5: they always share someone. This overlap argument is behind every safety rule later.]

== 9. Electing a leader, naively

#meta[Methods · the artifact, rebuilt live · Problem · about 1:00 min]

#opening[We need a leader. How do we pick one?]

#click(1)[No leader yet. A server that hears nothing for a while becomes a candidate and asks for votes.][If a server hears nothing for a while, it assumes there is no leader and runs for election.]
#click(2)[Each election has a number, the term. A server votes at most once per term; a majority of votes wins.][Terms are like election numbers. One vote per server per term, and a majority wins.]
#click(3)[Everyone uses the same timeout…][Now give every server the same timeout. Watch the timer rings.]
#click(4)[…so everyone becomes a candidate at once and votes for itself. Split vote. Again. Forever.][Everyone times out together, everyone votes for itself, nobody gets three votes. The term number keeps climbing. This never ends.]

#watch[Term numbers climbing on all five servers; the events list.]

#background[Watch the term number climb on every server while nobody wins. Each candidate already voted for itself, so it rejects everyone else.]

== 10. Randomized election timeouts

#meta[Methods · the artifact, rebuilt live · Fix · about 1:30 min]

#opening[The fix is almost embarrassingly simple.]

#click(1)[Each server picks a random timeout (here 2.5–7.5 s).][Each server picks a random timeout. Point at the rings: they now run out at different times.]
#click(2)[The first to wake up usually collects a majority before anyone else times out.][The first one to wake up asks for votes, and the others vote for it before their own timers run out.]
#click(3)[The leader sends heartbeats, so followers keep resetting their timers.][The leader keeps sending small heartbeat messages. Each one refills the followers' timers, so no new election starts.]
#click(4)[Kill the leader: followers time out and elect a new one in a higher term.][Now kill the leader. Heartbeats stop, a timer runs out, and we get a new leader in a higher term.]

#watch[The crown; rings refilling with each heartbeat; the term going up after the crash.]

#background[Ring around each follower \= its timer. Heartbeats refill it. You can click servers yourself here.]

== 11. Replicating the log, fire-and-forget

#meta[Methods · the artifact, rebuilt live · Problem · about 1:00 min]

#opening[We have a leader. Now it has to copy its log to the followers.]

#click(1)[S1 is leader. It appends each client command and sends it to every follower.][Simplest version: send each new command once and hope.]
#click(2)[The network is unreliable: some messages are lost.][Point at the red crosses: those messages were lost.]
#click(3)[Followers append whatever arrives, so one lost message shifts everything after it.][Followers just append what arrives, so after one lost message everything later sits at the wrong index.]
#click(4)[Logs (red outlines) and state machines no longer agree.][Red outlines in the log table, red values in the state machines. The copies have diverged.]

#watch[Red crosses on the links; S3 and S5 rows.]

#background[S3 missed the 2nd write, S5 the 3rd. Their later entries are at the wrong index.]

== 12. Say where it goes, acknowledge, retry

#meta[Methods · the artifact, rebuilt live · Fix · about 1:30 min]

#opening[Same lost messages as before, three small changes.]

#click(1)[Each AppendEntries names the previous entry (index + term); a follower only appends if it has that entry.][Every message says what entry comes right before the new ones. If the follower doesn't have that entry, it refuses.]
#click(2)[Followers reply. The leader tracks each follower's nextIndex (yellow underline).][Followers answer. The yellow underline is where the leader thinks each follower is.]
#click(3)[No reply? The leader resends from nextIndex with its next heartbeat.][No answer means the leader sends it again with the next heartbeat.]
#click(4)[Lost messages now cause delay, never divergence.][Same losses as last slide, but now every log ends up identical. Lost messages only cost time.]

#watch[Logs converging; all state machines equal at the end.]

#background[Same message losses as the previous slide. The heartbeat doubles as the retry timer.]

== 13. Applying too early

#meta[Methods · the artifact, rebuilt live · Problem · about 1:30 min]

#opening[So far a server applies an entry as soon as it has it. That is a problem.]

#click(1)[Right now a server applies an entry the moment it's in its log, and the leader answers the client immediately.][Look at the bottom panel: that's what the client was promised.]
#click(2)[A client writes. S1 applies it and says OK… its messages are lost… and it crashes.][Client writes, S1 applies it and says OK, its messages get lost, and S1 crashes.]
#click(3)[A new leader is elected. It never saw that entry.][Wait for the new leader. It never saw that write.]
#click(4)[The next write lands at the same index. The client was told OK, and the write is gone.][The next write takes the same spot in the log. Point at the receipt: struck out, LOST. The client was told OK and the data is gone.]
#click(5)[When S1 returns, its entry is overwritten, but it already applied it.][Bring S1 back: its log gets fixed, but its state machine already applied the lost value.]

#watch[The "Client was told OK" panel.]

#background[The receipts panel shows what the client believes happened. The struck-out one is a broken promise.]

== 14. Commit before you apply

#meta[Methods · the artifact, rebuilt live · Fix · about 1:30 min]

#opening[The fix: a write only counts once a majority has it.]

#click(1)[An entry is committed once a majority stores it. Only committed entries are applied, and only then does the client get OK.][Committed means stored on a majority. Only then apply it, and only then tell the client OK.]
#click(2)[Same story: S1 takes a write, its messages are lost, it crashes.][Exact same scenario as before.]
#click(3)[It never committed, so the client never got OK and will retry. No promise was broken.][S1 never got a majority, so the client never got OK. The client knows to retry. No broken promise.]
#click(4)[Followers learn the commit index from the leader's next AppendEntries (faded \= not yet committed).][Faded entries are not yet committed. Followers learn the commit point from the leader's next message.]

#watch[Faded vs solid cells; the receipts panel stays clean.]

#background[Uncommitted entries are drawn faded and dashed. Majority storage means any future leader will have it (we'll need one more rule for that).]

== 15. Catching up a returning server

#meta[Methods · the artifact, rebuilt live · Demo · about 1:00 min]

#opening[What happens when a server comes back after being away?]

#click(1)[S4 was offline while entries 3–6 were committed, and S1 has just become leader.][S4 missed four entries, and S1 became leader while it was gone.]
#click(2)[A new leader doesn't know how far behind anyone is: it guesses nextIndex \= end of its own log.][A new leader just guesses: everyone is up to date.]
#click(3)[S4 comes back. AppendEntries is rejected: S4 doesn't have the previous entry.][S4 comes back and says no: it doesn't have the entry before the new ones.]
#click(4)[The leader steps nextIndex back one at a time until the logs match…][Point at the yellow underline in S4's row: it steps left, one entry per rejection.]
#click(5)[…then streams the missing entries, and S4 rebuilds its state machine from the log.][Once they match, the leader sends everything S4 is missing, and S4 rebuilds its key-value store.]

#watch[S4's row and its yellow underline walking left.]

#background[Watch the yellow underline in S4's row walk left. Real implementations skip back faster using a hint from the follower.]

== 16. The wrong server wins

#meta[Methods · the artifact, rebuilt live · Problem · about 1:30 min]

#opening[Now a nasty one. Look at the logs: S4 and S5 are missing three committed entries.]

#click(1)[S4 and S5 were down while S1–S3 committed entries 4–6 (term 2).][They were down while S1 to S3 committed entries 4 to 6.]
#click(2)[S1 crashes just as S4 and S5 return. S5 times out first…][S1 crashes, S4 and S5 come back, and S5 times out first.]
#click(3)[…nobody looks at S5's log, so it wins the election.][Nobody checks how complete S5's log is, so it gets votes and wins.]
#click(4)[Its next entry overwrites committed entries on S2 and S3.][Its next entry overwrites committed entries on S2 and S3. Red banner, and the receipts show the client's data is lost. Committed data must never disappear.]

#watch[The red banner; LOST receipts.]

#background[Committed meant 'on a majority', but the new leader wasn't part of that majority. Committed data must never be lost.]

== 17. Vote only for up-to-date logs

#meta[Methods · the artifact, rebuilt live · Fix · about 1:30 min]

#opening[Fix: be picky about who you vote for.]

#click(1)[Refuse to vote for a candidate whose log is behind yours: compare the last entry's term, then log length.][Compare the last entry: higher term wins; same term, longer log wins. Never vote for someone behind you.]
#click(2)[Same story: S1 crashes, S4 and S5 return, S5 times out first…][Same story as before.]
#click(3)[S2 and S3 refuse. S5 can't reach a majority.][S2 and S3 refuse. Point at the events list: "refuses vote, log is behind".]
#click(4)[S2 or S3 wins, and every committed entry survives.][So S2 or S3 becomes leader, and nothing committed is lost. If it takes a while, shift-click S2.]
#click(5)[Why it works: committed \= on a majority; elected \= votes from a majority. They overlap.][The reason it works: committed means on a majority, winning needs votes from a majority, and those two majorities share a server that refuses.]

#watch["refuses vote" lines in the events; no red banner.]

#background[This is the 'election restriction' from §5.4.1 of the paper. S4\/S5 keep timing out and bumping the term, which can delay the election; the 4th step nudges S2's timer (shift-click works too).]

== 18. Two leaders? Network partitions

#meta[Methods · the artifact, rebuilt live · Demo · about 1:30 min]

#opening[Last failure: the network splits.]

#click(1)[The network splits: S1, S2 | S3, S4, S5.][Red dashed links: S1 and S2 can't talk to the other three.]
#click(2)[S1 still thinks it is leader and accepts a write, but it can't reach a majority, so it never commits.][S1 still thinks it's leader and accepts a write, but only S2 can store it, so it stays faded and uncommitted. The client never gets OK.]
#click(3)[The majority side elects a leader in a higher term and commits new writes.][The other side elects its own leader in a higher term. Two crowns at once. That's fine: only one can commit.]
#click(4)[The network heals. S1 sees the higher term, steps down, and its uncommitted entry is replaced.][Heal it. S1 sees the higher term, steps down, and its uncommitted entry is replaced. Terms tell everyone which leader is current.]

#watch[Two crowns; S1's faded entry being replaced.]

#background[Two crowns at once is fine: only one of them can ever commit. Terms tell everyone which leader is current.]

== 19. The subtle one (Figure 8) #opt

#meta[Methods · the artifact, rebuilt live · Bonus problem · about 1:30 min]

#opening[Optional, a famous corner case from the paper, Figure 8.]

#click(1)[Earlier: S1 wrote entry 2 in term 2; S5 wrote a different entry 2 in term 3 and went down. S1 is leader again in term 4.][The history is already set up: two short-lived leaders wrote different entries at index 2.]
#click(2)[S1 copies its old term-2 entry to a majority and counts it as committed.][S1 copies its old entry to a majority and calls it committed.]
#click(3)[S1 crashes. S5 returns: its last term (3) beats everyone's (2), so it is allowed to win…][S1 crashes. S5's last entry has term 3, newer than everyone's term 2, so it is allowed to win.]
#click(4)[…and overwrites the “committed” entry 2 everywhere.][And it overwrites the entry we called committed. Red banner.]

#background[Figure 8 in the paper. Counting replicas is not enough for entries from an older term.]

== 20. Only count replicas for the current term #opt

#meta[Methods · the artifact, rebuilt live · Bonus fix · about 1:00 min]

#opening[Optional. The fix is one more rule.]

#click(1)[A leader only commits by counting replicas for entries from its own term.][Only count copies for entries from your own term.]
#click(2)[S1 copies entry 2 to a majority, but it stays uncommitted.][S1 copies entry 2 to a majority, but does not call it committed yet.]
#click(3)[S1 commits a new term-4 entry; that commits entry 2 with it.][S1 commits a new entry from its own term; everything before it becomes committed too.]
#click(4)[Now S5's last term (3) is behind the majority's (4): it can't win.][Now S5 is behind the majority and can't win.]

#background[Older entries get committed indirectly, via the log matching property.]

== 21. How the authors evaluated Raft

#meta[Methods · evaluation · Methods · about 1:00 min]

#opening[That was the algorithm. Now: how did the authors show it is actually better?]

#click(1)[Understandability: a user study. Each student watched a lecture on both algorithms and took a quiz on each; half saw Paxos first.][43 students at Stanford and Berkeley, video lectures and a quiz on each algorithm. Half did Paxos first, so order doesn't decide the result.]
#click(2)[Correctness: a formal specification and proofs.][A formal TLA+ specification and proofs.]
#click(3)[Performance: their own implementation, repeatedly crashing the leader of a 5-server cluster and timing the election.][Their own implementation: crash the leader again and again and time the election.]

#background[Section 9 of the paper. Note the study was designed to favor Paxos where it could: 15 of 43 had prior Paxos experience, and the Paxos video was 14% longer.]

== 22. Students understood Raft better

#meta[Results · Results · about 1:30 min]

#opening[The first result: understandability. This is the paper's own figure.]

#click(1)[43 students at Stanford and U.C. Berkeley; about half learned Paxos first, half Raft first.][Two video lectures and two quizzes per student. Half started with Paxos, so the order can't decide the result.]
#click(2)[Average quiz score out of 60: 20.8 for Paxos, 25.7 for Raft. 33 of 43 did better on Raft.][Point at the diagonal: every point above it is a student who did better on Raft. That's 33 of 43.]
#click(3)[t-test: with 95% confidence, Raft's mean is at least 2.5 points higher.][So the difference is statistically significant, not luck.]
